@PACK Dutch Oven
@ENTRY seasoning|Seasoning and Care
A camp Dutch oven is bare cast iron, so it lives and dies by its seasoning: a baked-on layer of oil that becomes a natural nonstick surface. To season, coat the clean oven and lid in a thin film of neutral oil, wipe off the excess, and bake it upside down until it stops smoking. Repeat for a few layers.

After cooking, clean with hot water and a stiff brush, never soap on a well-seasoned oven. Dry it completely over low heat, then rub in a thin coat of oil while warm. Store the lid slightly ajar so no moisture is trapped inside, which is what causes rust.

If rust appears, scrub it off, wash, dry, and re-season. Cast iron is nearly indestructible; a neglected oven can always be brought back.
@END
@ENTRY coals|Counting Coals for Temperature
The genius of a camp Dutch oven is the flanged lid that holds coals on top, so heat surrounds the food like a real oven. Charcoal briquettes give predictable, even heat, far easier to control than wood coals.

A simple rule sets roughly 350F: take the oven's diameter in inches and use that many coals total, plus a few. For a 12-inch oven that's about 24 coals. Put about two-thirds on the lid and one-third underneath. More coals on top than bottom prevents burning the food and browns the surface, which is what you want for most baking.

To bake (cobblers, cakes, breads), put more heat on top. To simmer or fry, put more heat on the bottom. Rotate the oven a third turn and the lid a third turn every 15 minutes so hot spots don't scorch one side.
@END
@ENTRY cheesychicken|Cheesy Chicken and Potatoes
This one-pot dinner is a camp crowd-pleaser and forgiving to make. You'll need boneless chicken (breasts or thighs) cut into chunks, about 6 medium potatoes diced small, a couple cups of shredded cheddar, and salt, pepper, and garlic powder.

Line the bottom of a 12-inch oven lightly with oil. Toss the diced potatoes with oil and seasoning and spread them in an even layer, then set the seasoned chicken chunks on top. Cover and bake with about 8 coals underneath and 16 on the lid (roughly 350F) for 40 to 50 minutes, rotating oven and lid every 15 minutes, until the potatoes are fork-tender and the chicken reaches 165F.

In the last 5 minutes, scatter the shredded cheese over the top and replace the lid until it melts. Optional: a handful of cooked bacon bits or a spoon of sour cream stirred in at the end. Serve straight from the oven.
@END
@ENTRY peachcobbler|Easy Peach Cobbler
The famous dump cobbler: three ingredients, almost impossible to ruin, and it tastes like you fussed for hours. You need two large cans of peach pie filling (or sliced peaches in syrup), one box of yellow cake mix, and one stick of butter. A can of soda works in place of butter if you prefer.

Grease a 12-inch oven or line it with parchment or foil. Pour the pie filling in and spread it flat. Sprinkle the dry cake mix evenly over the top, do not stir. Slice the cold butter thin and lay it across the cake mix, covering as much as you can (or pour the soda evenly over the dry mix).

Bake with about 8 coals underneath and 16 on the lid for 30 to 40 minutes, rotating every 15, until the top is golden and the filling bubbles up at the edges. Any dry spots of cake mix will set as it cools. Serve warm, wonderful with a scoop of ice cream if you packed a cooler.
@END
@ENTRY streuselcake|Coffee Streusel Cake
A warm crumb-topped cake makes camp mornings feel like a bakery. Use one box of yellow or spice cake mix prepared per its directions (eggs, oil, water, mixed in a bag or bowl). For the streusel, mix about a cup of brown sugar, half a cup of flour, a couple teaspoons of cinnamon, and a few tablespoons of softened butter into coarse crumbs.

Grease and line a 12-inch oven. Pour in half the batter, sprinkle half the streusel, add the rest of the batter, and top with the remaining streusel. This gives you a ribbon of cinnamon crumb through the middle and a crunchy top.

Bake with about 8 coals underneath and 16 on the lid (350F) for 30 to 40 minutes, rotating oven and lid every 15, until a stick poked in the center comes out clean. Let it cool a few minutes before cutting. A simple glaze of powdered sugar and a splash of water drizzled on top is worth the small effort.
@END
@ENTRY breakfast|Mountain Man Breakfast
A hearty all-in-one breakfast bake that feeds a hungry group. You need a bag of frozen hash browns or diced potatoes, a pound of breakfast sausage or bacon, a dozen eggs, shredded cheese, and salt and pepper.

Brown the sausage or bacon in the bottom of a 12-inch oven set over coals, then drain most of the grease. Spread the potatoes over the meat and cook until they start to soften. Beat the eggs with salt and pepper and pour them over everything.

Cover and bake with about 10 coals underneath and 14 on top for 20 to 30 minutes, until the eggs are set. Top with cheese in the last few minutes and let it melt. Serve with tortillas or toast. Diced onion and peppers are a nice addition if your group eats them.
@END
@ENTRY tips|Dutch Oven Tips and Tricks
Line the oven with parchment or heavy foil for sweets and sticky bakes; cleanup becomes a two-second job and your seasoning stays intact. A trivet or a few small stones on the bottom lifts baked goods off direct heat and prevents a scorched underside.

A lid lifter or sturdy pliers and heavy gloves are not optional, the lid and coals are dangerous to handle bare. Flip the hot lid over on a couple of rocks to make an instant griddle for eggs or pancakes while the main dish bakes below.

Wind and cold steal heat, so add a few coals on breezy or chilly days and shelter the oven. Refresh spent coals before they die: dead gray coals give no heat. And always let the oven cool gradually; dunking hot cast iron in cold water can crack it.
@END
